<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gertrude of Merania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1185–<a href="/years/1213">1213</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">House of Andechs</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gertrude of Merania (c. 1185–1213) was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> as the first wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II</a>, and a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berthold-iv-duke-of-merania">Berthold IV, Duke of Merania</a>. Her favour towards her German relatives and courtiers caused resentment among the Hungarian nobility, and she was murdered by conspirators in 1213 while Andrew was on campaign. Her children included King Béla IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Saint Elizabeth of Hungary</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gertrude-of-merania.jpg" alt="Gertrude of Merania" width="260" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Caro1409 on de.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andreas_Getrude_Ungarn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gertrude was the daughter of the Bavarian Count Berthold IV of Andechs, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> and Istria, and of Agnes of the Saxon House of Wettin. Her elder sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnes-of-merania">Agnes of Merania</a> married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>, and her younger sister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedwig-of-silesia">Saint Hedwig of Silesia</a>, married the Piast duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry I the Bearded</a>. Among her brothers, Otto inherited the title of Duke of Merania, Henry took over Carniola and Istria, and Berthold became Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalocsa">Kalocsa</a> and a close adviser to her. Her parents arranged political marriages for their daughters, and Gertrude married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II</a>, younger son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iii-of-hungary">Béla III of Hungary</a>, before 1204.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Andrew's marriage drew him into the conflict over the German throne: he joined his father-in-law in backing Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a>, while his elder brother King Emeric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> supported King Otto IV. Gertrude exerted much political influence over her husband, and it was probably she who persuaded him to conspire again against Emeric. Emeric went unarmed to Andrew's camp near Varasd and Andrew surrendered, was arrested and soon escaped, and Gertrude was sent back to her father for a time. Andrew took over the government on Emeric's death in 1204, officially as regent for his nephew Ladislaus III, who died in exile a year later.</p><p>As queen from 1205 she was regent during her husband's frequent absences, and the chronicler Dietrich of Apolda says she ran the kingdom like a man. Medieval chroniclers say she gave Hungarian land to her favourites, as much as a third of the country, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> recovered after her death. Her brother Berthold was made Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalocsa">Kalocsa</a> in 1206 and Voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in 1212. She and Andrew had five children, among them King Béla IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Saint Elizabeth of Hungary</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>While Andrew was campaigning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, a Hungarian noble, Peter, son of Töre, assassinated the queen in 1213, during a hunt in the Pilis Mountains with Berthold and their guest Duke Leopold VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Her body was torn to pieces, while her brother and Leopold narrowly escaped. Recent research suggests the killer may have acted alone with a personal motive, and that Andrew, on learning of it, had him impaled. Decades later Béla IV used the killing for political purposes. Andrew then married Yolanda de Courtenay.</p><p>Gertrude is the villain of József Katona's play Bánk bán (1819) and Ferenc Erkel's opera of 1861, retellings in which Hungarian nobles conspire against her. They are strongly fictionalised: there are no reliable sources on the assassination, and no proof that she was an exploitative regent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gertrude of Merania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Berthold IV's daughters married widely: Agnes became Queen of France, <b>Gertrude</b> Queen of Hungary and mother of Saint Elizabeth, and Hedwig Duchess of Silesia, later Saint Hedwig.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-merania">Duchy of Merania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Andechs, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Pilis Mountains, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.97,11.19&amp;year=1203" aria-label="Andechs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4810 484 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gertrude of Merania"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5260 754h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5260 754h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.97,11.19&amp;year=1203">What was here in 1203? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
